1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,682
Four balloons tied at one knot.

2
00:00:02,682 --> 00:00:04,325
A bent water molecule.

3
00:00:04,325 --> 00:00:06,701
A straight carbon dioxide molecule.

4
00:00:07,101 --> 00:00:08,001
Three shapes.

5
00:00:07,853 --> 00:00:09,673
One simple rule decides all of them.

6
00:00:10,123 --> 00:00:13,057
Diya sees it while decorating
for her sister's birthday.

7
00:00:13,057 --> 00:00:15,063
She ties four balloons at one knot.

8
00:00:15,413 --> 00:00:16,902
They never lie flat.

9
00:00:16,902 --> 00:00:21,220
They push apart and point to four
corners, like a little pyramid.

10
00:00:21,720 --> 00:00:25,762
By the end, you will predict the
shape of a molecule just by counting.

11
00:00:26,412 --> 00:00:28,349
So what decides the shape of a molecule?

12
00:00:28,699 --> 00:00:30,838
Every molecule has a central atom.

13
00:00:30,838 --> 00:00:32,932
Around it sit pairs of electrons.

14
00:00:33,282 --> 00:00:35,350
Some pairs hold another atom.

15
00:00:35,350 --> 00:00:37,160
We call them bond pairs.

16
00:00:37,510 --> 00:00:39,678
Some pairs hold no atom at all.

17
00:00:39,678 --> 00:00:41,386
We call them lone pairs.

18
00:00:41,736 --> 00:00:46,169
All these pairs are negative, and
negative charges push each other away.

19
00:00:46,169 --> 00:00:47,725
Just like the balloons.

20
00:00:48,125 --> 00:00:51,528
So the pairs spread out,
as far apart as they can.

21
00:00:51,528 --> 00:00:53,177
That is the whole idea.

22
00:00:53,827 --> 00:00:55,954
This idea is called VSEPR theory.

23
00:00:55,954 --> 00:00:57,884
Valence Shell Electron Pair Repulsion.

24
00:00:57,884 --> 00:01:00,027
Sidgwick and Powell suggested it in 1940.

25
00:01:00,027 --> 00:01:02,170
Nyholm and Gillespie improved it in 1957.

26
00:01:02,820 --> 00:01:05,114
Let's watch it on our molecule bench.

27
00:01:05,114 --> 00:01:07,548
Start with two pairs around the centre.

28
00:01:07,898 --> 00:01:09,973
Two pairs push to opposite sides.

29
00:01:09,973 --> 00:01:11,901
One hundred and eighty degrees.

30
00:01:11,901 --> 00:01:15,326
A straight line, called linear,
like beryllium chloride.

31
00:01:15,726 --> 00:01:18,041
Three pairs spread around a flat circle.

32
00:01:18,041 --> 00:01:19,862
One hundred and twenty degrees.

33
00:01:19,862 --> 00:01:22,160
Trigonal planar, like boron trifluoride.

34
00:01:22,560 --> 00:01:24,457
Four pairs cannot stay flat.

35
00:01:24,457 --> 00:01:25,808
They jump into 3D.

36
00:01:25,808 --> 00:01:28,405
One hundred and nine point five degrees.

37
00:01:28,405 --> 00:01:30,340
A tetrahedron, like methane.

38
00:01:30,740 --> 00:01:32,743
The solid wedge comes towards you.

39
00:01:32,743 --> 00:01:34,971
The dashed one goes behind the screen.

40
00:01:35,371 --> 00:01:36,271
Five pairs.

41
00:01:36,172 --> 00:01:40,617
Three around the middle at one hundred
and twenty, two up and down at ninety.

42
00:01:40,617 --> 00:01:43,450
Trigonal bipyramidal, like
phosphorus pentachloride.

43
00:01:43,850 --> 00:01:44,750
Six pairs.

44
00:01:44,524 --> 00:01:46,267
Every neighbour at ninety degrees.

45
00:01:46,267 --> 00:01:48,195
Octahedral, like sulphur hexafluoride.

46
00:01:48,845 --> 00:01:51,373
Now swap a bond pair for a lone pair.

47
00:01:51,373 --> 00:01:56,266
A lone pair is held by one atom only,
so it spreads wider and pushes harder.

48
00:01:56,666 --> 00:02:00,980
Methane has four bond pairs, at
one hundred and nine point five.

49
00:02:00,980 --> 00:02:02,849
Ammonia has one lone pair.

50
00:02:02,849 --> 00:02:05,758
Its angle shrinks to
one hundred and seven.

51
00:02:06,158 --> 00:02:07,180
Pause and think.

52
00:02:07,180 --> 00:02:08,675
Water has two lone pairs.

53
00:02:08,675 --> 00:02:11,408
Will its angle be bigger
or smaller than ammonia's?

54
00:02:14,608 --> 00:02:15,508
Smaller!

55
00:02:15,193 --> 00:02:17,016
Two lone pairs push even harder.

56
00:02:17,016 --> 00:02:20,026
Water's angle is one hundred
and four point five degrees.

57
00:02:20,676 --> 00:02:23,893
Here is the pushing
order, one link at a time.

58
00:02:24,243 --> 00:02:25,143
Link one.

59
00:02:24,987 --> 00:02:27,513
Lone pair with lone pair pushes the most.

60
00:02:28,263 --> 00:02:29,163
Link two.

61
00:02:29,013 --> 00:02:31,273
Lone pair with bond pair comes next.

62
00:02:32,023 --> 00:02:32,923
Link three.

63
00:02:32,868 --> 00:02:35,469
Bond pair with bond pair pushes the least.

64
00:02:36,219 --> 00:02:37,119
Link four.

65
00:02:37,014 --> 00:02:40,316
A double or triple bond still
counts as one pair here.

66
00:02:40,316 --> 00:02:41,587
It points one way.

67
00:02:42,337 --> 00:02:43,237
Last link.

68
00:02:43,186 --> 00:02:44,980
Geometry counts every pair.

69
00:02:44,980 --> 00:02:47,546
Shape names only the atoms we can see.

70
00:02:47,546 --> 00:02:51,154
Water's pairs make a tetrahedron,
but its shape is bent.

71
00:02:51,904 --> 00:02:52,931
Remember this.

72
00:02:52,931 --> 00:02:56,672
Electron pairs push apart, and
lone pairs push the hardest.

73
00:02:57,372 --> 00:02:58,716
Now, one worked example.

74
00:02:58,716 --> 00:03:00,833
What is the shape of
sulphur tetrafluoride?

75
00:03:01,133 --> 00:03:02,033
Step one.

76
00:03:01,870 --> 00:03:03,937
Sulphur brings six outer electrons.

77
00:03:03,937 --> 00:03:05,779
Each fluorine shares one more.

78
00:03:05,779 --> 00:03:07,217
Six plus four is ten.

79
00:03:07,967 --> 00:03:08,867
Step two.

80
00:03:08,588 --> 00:03:10,141
Ten electrons make five pairs.

81
00:03:10,891 --> 00:03:11,791
Step three.

82
00:03:11,651 --> 00:03:13,131
Four pairs hold fluorines.

83
00:03:13,131 --> 00:03:14,801
So one pair is a lone pair.

84
00:03:15,551 --> 00:03:16,451
Step four.

85
00:03:16,340 --> 00:03:18,569
Five pairs make a trigonal bipyramid.

86
00:03:18,569 --> 00:03:22,874
The lone pair takes a seat in the
middle ring, where it has more room.

87
00:03:23,624 --> 00:03:25,346
Now look only at the atoms.

88
00:03:25,346 --> 00:03:27,604
The four fluorines look like a see-saw.

89
00:03:27,604 --> 00:03:29,098
So the shape is see-saw.

90
00:03:29,748 --> 00:03:32,945
The same counting gives
every shape with lone pairs.

91
00:03:33,345 --> 00:03:35,372
Three pairs, one lone pair.

92
00:03:35,372 --> 00:03:37,339
Bent, like sulphur dioxide.

93
00:03:37,739 --> 00:03:39,419
Five pairs, two lone pairs.

94
00:03:39,419 --> 00:03:41,385
T-shaped, like chlorine trifluoride.

95
00:03:41,785 --> 00:03:43,378
Six pairs, one lone pair.

96
00:03:43,378 --> 00:03:45,725
Square pyramid, like
bromine pentafluoride.

97
00:03:46,125 --> 00:03:47,832
Six pairs, two lone pairs.

98
00:03:47,832 --> 00:03:50,126
The lone pairs sit opposite each other.

99
00:03:50,126 --> 00:03:52,447
Square planar, like xenon tetrafluoride.

100
00:03:53,097 --> 00:03:54,560
Now, the exam trap.

101
00:03:54,910 --> 00:03:57,194
Ammonia has three
hydrogens around nitrogen.

102
00:03:57,194 --> 00:04:00,356
So students call it trigonal
planar, like boron trifluoride.

103
00:04:00,756 --> 00:04:01,656
Wrong.

104
00:04:01,338 --> 00:04:03,695
Count the lone pair on nitrogen too.

105
00:04:03,695 --> 00:04:08,220
Four pairs, so it is trigonal pyramidal,
with an angle of one hundred and

106
00:04:08,220 --> 00:04:09,120
seven.

107
00:04:09,452 --> 00:04:10,753
Where is this asked?

108
00:04:10,753 --> 00:04:12,237
Both JEE Main and NEET.

109
00:04:12,237 --> 00:04:15,363
Matching molecules to shapes,
and ordering bond angles.

110
00:04:15,963 --> 00:04:16,983
Exam level now.

111
00:04:16,983 --> 00:04:19,378
Which of these molecules is square planar?

112
00:04:19,378 --> 00:04:20,441
Pause and solve.

113
00:04:25,841 --> 00:04:27,003
The answer is B.

114
00:04:27,003 --> 00:04:28,237
Xenon tetrafluoride.

115
00:04:28,737 --> 00:04:31,449
Xenon brings eight electrons,
and four fluorines add four.

116
00:04:31,449 --> 00:04:32,988
Twelve electrons make six pairs.

117
00:04:33,488 --> 00:04:35,674
Four bond pairs and two lone pairs.

118
00:04:35,674 --> 00:04:40,001
The lone pairs sit opposite, so the
four fluorines lie flat in a square.

119
00:04:40,501 --> 00:04:43,450
Sulphur tetrafluoride is a see-saw,
carbon tetrachloride a tetrahedron,

120
00:04:43,450 --> 00:04:45,168
bromine pentafluoride a square pyramid.

121
00:04:45,168 --> 00:04:47,746
A traps students who see four
fluorines and stop counting.

122
00:04:48,346 --> 00:04:49,886
Here is your recap.

123
00:04:49,886 --> 00:04:51,468
Screenshot this card.

124
00:04:54,668 --> 00:04:56,854
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

125
00:04:57,254 --> 00:04:58,154
One.

126
00:04:57,669 --> 00:05:00,438
Why does a lone pair push
harder than a bond pair?

127
00:05:01,238 --> 00:05:02,138
Two.

128
00:05:01,689 --> 00:05:05,145
Why is water bent, when its
four pairs make a tetrahedron?

129
00:05:05,945 --> 00:05:06,845
Three.

130
00:05:06,375 --> 00:05:09,055
How many lone pairs does xenon
have in xenon tetrafluoride?

131
00:05:09,955 --> 00:05:13,886
Next time: how do two atoms
actually hold on to each other?

132
00:05:13,886 --> 00:05:17,406
We find out in valence bond
theory and orbital overlap.
